+# 06: New copies go to the queue
+
+**Spec:** `.scratch/holds/spec.md`
+
+**What to build:** A new copy added to a Title that people are waiting for goes straight to the first person in the queue, so nobody can take it from the shelf ahead of them. `add_copy(copy_id, isbn)` keeps its signature and its existing checks (`TitleNotFound`, `DuplicateCopy`). It then places the new copy through the set-aside rule from ticket 02:
+
+- If the Title has a Waiting Hold, the copy goes to `ON_HOLD_SHELF`. The first Waiting Hold becomes Ready with that copy and a Pickup deadline counted from today, and the returned Copy shows `ON_HOLD_SHELF`.
+- Otherwise the copy is `AVAILABLE`, as today.
+
+With this in place, an `AVAILABLE` copy and a Waiting Hold never exist together on the same Title.
+
+**Blocked by:** 02 (A returned copy goes to the Hold shelf).
+
+**Status:** ready-for-agent
+
+- [ ] With `D1` out and a Hold waiting on `dvd-1`, adding copy `D2` returns it as `ON_HOLD_SHELF`. The Hold is `READY` with `copy_id` `D2` and `pickup_by` 7 days from today (moved past a closed day).
+- [ ] With two Holds waiting, only the first becomes Ready when one copy is added.
+- [ ] Adding a copy to a Title with nobody waiting gives an `AVAILABLE` copy, as before. That includes a Title whose only Hold is already Ready.
+- [ ] `add_copy` still raises `TitleNotFound` and `DuplicateCopy` as before, and touches no Hold when it does.
+- [ ] Tests drive only the public `LendingService` interface with the shared fixtures and the `FixedClock`.
+- [ ] All existing tests still pass.
