+# Overdue fines and member Accounts
+
+Status: ready-for-agent
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Returning a copy late costs the member nothing, so copies come back weeks late. DVDs are the worst, with a 7-day loan period they routinely ignore. Front-desk staff have no way to record what a member owes: not for late returns, and not for anything else, such as a damaged case. So there is nothing to stop a member who owes the library money from borrowing more.
+
+## Solution
+
+Every member gets an **Account**. Returning a copy late automatically raises a **Fine** on it, based on the number of **Late days**. Staff can raise a **Manual charge** with a reason, and members can make **Payments**. The member's **Balance** is what they owe right now. A member whose Balance reaches 10.00 is **Blocked**: checkout is refused until they pay it down. Children are never fined, but they can receive Manual charges and be Blocked like anyone else.
+
+Vocabulary follows `GLOSSARY.md`. The timing of Fines follows ADR-0001 (Fines are posted when lateness ends, not while it builds up).
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a front-desk staff member, I want a late return to charge the member automatically, so that I don't have to work out and enter fines by hand.
+2. As a front-desk staff member, I want returning a copy to work exactly as before (same call, same Loan handed back), so that the desk workflow doesn't change.
+3. As a library manager, I want a late DVD to cost 1.00 per Late day, so that members have a real reason to bring DVDs back on time.
+4. As a library manager, I want a late book to cost 0.25 per Late day, so that book fines stay modest but still discourage lateness.
+5. As a member, I want Sundays not to count as Late days, so that I'm not fined for days when the library was closed and I couldn't have returned the copy.
+6. As a member, I want a copy returned on or before its due date to cost nothing, so that returning on time is never penalised.
+7. As a member, I want a copy handed back on a closed day just after its due date to cost nothing if no open day has passed, so that I'm not fined when the library gave me no chance to return it.
+8. As a library manager, I want lateness to count from the first day after the due date with no grace period, so that the rule is simple to explain at the desk.
+9. As a member, I want a Loan's Fines never to exceed the replacement cost of the Title, so that a very late return never costs more than the copy is worth.
+10. As a parent, I want my child never to be fined for a late return, so that children are still encouraged to use the library.
+11. As a front-desk staff member, I want a child's late return to be handled like any other return (Loan closed, copy shelved), so that the only difference is that nothing is charged.
+12. As a front-desk staff member, I want to look up a member's Balance, so that I can tell them what they owe.
+13. As a newly registered member, I want my Balance to start at 0.00, so that I owe nothing until something is charged.
+14. As a front-desk staff member, I want to raise a Manual charge with a reason, such as a damaged case, so that costs other than late returns are recorded too.
+15. As a front-desk staff member, I want raising a Manual charge to tell me the member's new Balance, so that I can tell them straight away.
+16. As a library manager, I want every Charge kept with its date and, for a Manual charge, its reason, so that staff can explain to a member why they owe what they owe.
+17. As a library manager, I want each Fine linked to the Loan it is for, so that a Fine can always be traced back to the copy that came back late.
+18. As a member, I want to pay all or part of my Balance, so that I can clear what I owe in one go or bit by bit.
+19. As a front-desk staff member, I want taking a Payment to tell me the new Balance, so that I can confirm it with the member at the desk.
+20. As a front-desk staff member, I want a Payment larger than the Balance to be refused, so that the library never holds money as credit for a member.
+21. As a front-desk staff member, I want zero, negative or sub-cent amounts to be refused for both Charges and Payments, so that typing mistakes don't end up on an Account.
+22. As a developer calling the service, I want float amounts to be refused, so that money is never handled as a float anywhere in the system.
+23. As a developer calling the service, I want NaN and infinite amounts to be refused, so that an Account can never end up with an amount that makes no sense.
+24. As a developer calling the service, I want unknown members to raise the same not-found error for Balance, Charges and Payments as they do everywhere else, so that error handling stays the same across the service.
+25. As a library manager, I want a member who owes 10.00 or more to be unable to borrow, so that members settle what they owe before taking out more copies.
+26. As a member who owes 9.99, I want to still be able to borrow, so that the block applies only at the threshold.
+27. As a member who was Blocked, I want to be able to borrow again as soon as a Payment brings my Balance below 10.00, so that paying is all it takes to unblock me.
+28. As a front-desk staff member, I want a Blocked member to be told about the block before anything about the copy (already on loan, or loan limit reached), so that we talk about the money first.
+29. As a developer calling the service, I want the block error to carry the member, their Balance and the threshold, so that the desk can show a clear message such as "owes 12.00, limit 10.00".
+30. As a library manager, I want the same 10.00 threshold for adults and children, so that there is one rule for borrowing.
+31. As a parent, I want my child to be Blocked only through Manual charges, so that the block reflects real costs like damage and never late returns.
+32. As a member with an overdue copy still at home, I want my Balance to show only what has actually been charged, so that the figure I'm quoted today doesn't change from one day to the next while nothing is recorded.
+33. As a library manager, I want the overdue report to stay exactly as it is (calendar days since the due date), so that the printout staff already use doesn't change.
+34. As a library manager, I want the fine rates and the borrowing threshold kept with the other circulation numbers, so that changing a rate is a one-line policy change.
+35. As a library manager, I want Late days to follow the library's own closed-day rule, so that if we ever close on another weekday, fines and due dates change together.
+36. As a developer extending the service later, I want the Account to allow more than one Fine on a Loan, with the replacement-cost cap applied to their total, so that renewals can charge lateness without the cap being broken.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+**Interface contract.** Other code will call exactly this, all on `LendingService`:
+
+- `return_copy(copy_id)` keeps its signature and return value (the closed Loan). If the Loan was late, it now also posts a Fine to the member's Account.
+- `balance(member_id: str) -> Decimal`: the member's current Balance.
+- `add_charge(member_id: str, amount: Decimal, reason: str) -> Decimal`: posts a Manual charge and returns the new Balance.
+- `pay(member_id: str, amount: Decimal) -> Decimal`: records a Payment and returns the new Balance.
+- `checkout(member_id, copy_id)` is unchanged in signature, but now raises `AccountBlocked` for a Blocked member.
+
+**New errors**, both subclasses of `LendingError`, in the errors module:
+
+- `AccountBlocked`: carries the member id, the member's Balance and the threshold that was reached, and has a readable message. It carries the threshold because a later hold feature will reuse this error with its own, lower threshold.
+- `InvalidAmount`: carries the rejected amount and has a readable message.
+- Unknown members keep raising `MemberNotFound` from every new method.
+
+**Policy module** (where the circulation numbers live):
+
+- Fine rate per Late day, by material: DVD 1.00, Book 0.25, as `Decimal`, kept next to the loan periods.
+- Borrowing threshold: 10.00, the same for every member category.
+- A function that counts Late days: the days after the due date, up to and including the return day, on which the library is open. It uses the existing open-day rule, not a hardcoded Sunday. A return on or before the due date gives 0. A function that turns a material and a number of Late days into a fine amount is optional but fits here.
+- Children are never fined. This can be a policy rule or a service rule. Either way it is a single rule, not scattered checks.
+- Worked example (must hold): a book due Monday 23 March 2026 and returned Monday 30 March 2026 covers 24–30 March, which is 7 days. Taking off Sunday 29 March leaves 6 Late days, so the Fine is 6 × 0.25 = 1.50.
+
+**Fine on return:**
+
+- Amount = Late days × the material's rate, then capped. A Loan's Fines together must never exceed the Title's `replacement_cost`, so the Fine posted is at most the replacement cost minus any Fines already on that Loan. Today a Loan has at most one Fine. The cap is still computed against the Loan's total so that future renewals (ADR-0001) can't break it.
+- A Fine is posted only if the capped amount is greater than zero. A child member's return, a return with 0 Late days, or a Loan already at the cap writes nothing to the Account.
+- The Fine is dated on the return day (today from the injected Clock) and records the Loan it belongs to.
+
+**Account model:**
+
+- The Account is a record of entries, not a stored number. Each entry is either a Charge or a Payment, and the Balance is worked out from them: total Charges minus total Payments.
+- A Charge is either a Fine (linked to its Loan) or a Manual charge (with its reason). Every entry has a positive `Decimal` amount and the date it was recorded on, taken from the Clock. Entries keep the order they were recorded in.
+- Each Member has an Account from registration, so a registered member with no entries has a Balance of 0.00. The Balance is returned as a `Decimal` to two places.
+- The repository gains storage for Account entries behind its existing style of interface. The service stays the only thing that changes state. The new entry types don't need to be exported from the package or exposed as public read API. Listing a member's Account history is out of scope.
+
+**Balance semantics (ADR-0001):** the Balance includes only Charges that have been posted. Fines still building up on open overdue Loans are not in it and do not affect whether a member is Blocked.
+
+**Amount validation**, the same for `add_charge` and `pay`. Each of these raises `InvalidAmount`:
+
+- anything that isn't a `Decimal`, including `float`, `int` and `str`;
+- NaN or infinite values;
+- zero or negative values;
+- values that aren't a whole number of cents. This is judged by value, so `Decimal("1.000")` is accepted but `Decimal("0.001")` is not;
+- for `pay` only, an amount greater than the current Balance (no credit, and the Balance never goes below zero). A Payment of exactly the Balance brings it to 0.00.
+
+The member is looked up first. An unknown member with an invalid amount gets `MemberNotFound`.
+
+**Manual charge reason:** stored as given, with no validation.
+
+**Checkout check order:** member lookup (`MemberNotFound`), then the block check (`AccountBlocked` when the Balance is ≥ 10.00), then the existing copy lookup, availability and loan-limit checks in their current order. A Blocked member therefore gets `AccountBlocked` even for a copy that doesn't exist or is already on loan.
+
+**Unchanged:** the overdue report and its calendar-day `days_overdue`, the loan periods, due-date calculation, loan limits, and the Loan model's existing fields and behaviour.
+
+**Docs:** the README's Concepts section gains short entries for Account, Fine, Manual charge, Payment, Balance and Blocked, consistent with `GLOSSARY.md`.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam: the public `LendingService` interface**, built the way the current suite builds it, with an `InMemoryRepository` and a `FixedClock` from the shared `service` and `clock` fixtures. Those fixtures already provide an adult (`ann`), a child (`kid`), a book title (replacement cost 18.00) with copies `B1` and `B2`, and a DVD title (replacement cost 12.00) with copy `D1`, starting on Monday 2 March 2026.
+- **A good test** drives the service the way the desk would (`checkout`, move the clock, `return_copy`, `add_charge`, `pay`) and asserts only on what the service returns or raises, mostly `balance()`. Tests don't reach into the repository, don't inspect Account entries, and don't call policy functions directly, so the Late-day calculation and the Account storage can be refactored freely.
+- **Prior art:** the tests for checkout and return, which use `clock.set(...)` and `clock.advance(...)` with explicit dates and `pytest.raises` for domain errors. The overdue-report tests show how dates relative to the start day are written.
+- **Behaviours to cover:**
+  - The book example: due 23 March, returned 30 March, Balance 1.50.
+  - A late DVD charged at 1.00 per Late day with Sundays excluded. For example, due Monday 9 March and returned Monday 16 March gives 6 Late days, so 6.00.
+  - Returns on the due date and before it cost nothing.
+  - A return on a Sunday immediately after a Saturday due date costs nothing.
+  - The cap: a very late DVD gives a Balance of exactly 12.00.
+  - A child's late return costs nothing, and the Loan is still closed and the copy shelved.
+  - `return_copy` still returns the same closed Loan.
+  - A new member's Balance is 0.00.
+  - `add_charge` and `pay` return the new Balance, and `balance()` agrees with them.
+  - A partial Payment, and a Payment of exactly the Balance.
+  - An overpayment, and any Payment when the Balance is 0.00, raise `InvalidAmount`.
+  - Each invalid-amount case (zero, negative, NaN, infinite, sub-cent, float, int) raises `InvalidAmount` for both methods.
+  - `MemberNotFound` from `balance`, `add_charge` and `pay`.
+  - `AccountBlocked` at exactly 10.00 and above, but not at 9.99, carrying the member, Balance and threshold.
+  - `AccountBlocked` takes precedence over `CopyNotAvailable` and `LoanLimitReached`.
+  - Borrowing works again after a Payment brings the Balance below 10.00.
+  - A child can be Blocked through Manual charges.
+  - An overdue copy still out on loan doesn't change the Balance.
+  - The existing tests still pass unchanged, including the overdue-report tests.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- **Holds**, and their lower 5.00 hold threshold (which will reuse `AccountBlocked`).
+- **No-show fees** for uncollected holds (a future kind of Charge; children exempt).
+- **Renewals**, including charging the Fine built up so far when an overdue Loan is renewed. ADR-0001 and the cap on a Loan's total Fines are already written with this in mind.
+- Waiving, reversing or refunding a Charge. For now only a Payment reduces a Balance.
+- Credit balances and overpayments.
+- Assigning Payments to particular Charges.
+- Blocking members because they are holding overdue copies, as opposed to because of their Balance.
+- Any API for listing a member's Account history or statement, and any Balance or fines report.
+- Validating the Manual charge reason.
+- Changing the overdue report or how it counts days.
+- Persistence other than the in-memory repository.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- **ADR-0001** (Fines are posted when lateness ends) is binding here. Don't add Fines still building up to the Balance. Any future operation that moves a due date must post the Fine built up so far, counted as if the copy had been returned that day.
+- Late days and the overdue report's `days_overdue` measure different things on purpose. Don't make them the same.
+- All dates come from the injected Clock. Package code never calls `date.today()`.
