+# 01: Renew an open Loan in place
+
+**Spec:** `.scratch/renewals/spec.md`
+
+**What to build:** Staff can renew a member's Loan instead of returning the copy and checking it out again. `LendingService.renew(loan_id) -> Loan` gives the same Loan a new due date and returns it.
+
+- The new due date is the due date of a checkout of this material today: a full loan period from the renewal day, moved to the next open day if it lands on a closed day.
+- The Loan keeps its `loan_id`, `copy_id`, member and `borrowed_on`, and stays open. The copy stays `ON_LOAN`.
+- `Loan` gains `renewals: int`, default `0`, which goes up by one on each renewal. It is the only new field.
+- A new error `RenewalRefused(LendingError)` carries the `loan_id` and a short, readable `reason`, and its message includes both.
+- An unknown id raises `LoanNotFound`. A returned Loan raises `RenewalRefused`, not `NoOpenLoan`, because its copy may since have gone out to someone else.
+- Renewing an overdue Loan first posts the Fine for the Late days up to and including today, dated today and linked to the Loan (ADR-0001). It uses the existing fine logic, so the replacement-cost cap applies to all the Loan's Fines together and children are never fined. Late days then count against the new due date. On or before the due date nothing is written.
+- The loan limit and the borrowing threshold are not checked.
+- The README's Concepts section gains a Renewal entry, consistent with `GLOSSARY.md`, and its Loan entry mentions the renewals count.
+
+The other refusals come in tickets 02–04. Tests here should renew within five days of the due date (for example on Thursday 19 March for a Loan due Monday 23 March), so they still pass once ticket 02 adds the window.
+
+**Blocked by:** None (can start immediately).
+
+**Status:** ready-for-agent
+
+- [x] A new checkout has `renewals` 0.
+- [x] A book borrowed Monday 2 March 2026 (due Monday 23 March) and renewed Thursday 19 March is returned as the same Loan with due Thursday 9 April, renewals 1, the same `loan_id`, `copy_id`, member and `borrowed_on`, and still open. `get_loan` shows the same.
+- [x] After a renewal, `loan_history` still holds one Loan, `open_loans` is unchanged and the copy is still `ON_LOAN`.
+- [x] Renewed on Sunday 22 March, the Loan is due Monday 13 April (21 days later is a Sunday).
+- [x] Renewing an overdue book on Saturday 28 March for an adult posts a Fine of 1.25 (Late days 24–28 March), dated 28 March and linked to the Loan. The Loan is due Saturday 18 April, and it no longer appears in `overdue_loans`.
+- [x] That book then returned on Tuesday 21 April posts a second Fine of 0.50 (20 and 21 April; the 19th is a Sunday), giving a Balance of 1.75.
+- [x] With a book whose replacement cost is 1.00, the renewal Fine is cut to 1.00, and a later late return posts no further Fine.
+- [x] A child renewing an overdue book is not fined, and their Balance stays 0.00.
+- [x] Renewing on or before the due date posts nothing and leaves the Balance unchanged.
+- [x] A member at their loan limit can renew.
+- [x] A returned Loan raises `RenewalRefused` carrying its `loan_id`, including after its copy has gone out again on someone else's Loan. That other Loan is untouched.
+- [x] An unknown loan id raises `LoanNotFound`.
+- [x] Tests drive only the public `LendingService` interface with the shared fixtures and the `FixedClock`.
+- [x] All existing tests still pass.
