+# Renewals
+
+Status: ready-for-agent
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Members often ask to keep a book longer than its loan period. There is no way to do that today. Staff work around it by returning the copy and checking it straight out again. That closes the Loan and opens a new one with a new id. The member's loan history then shows two Loans for what was one borrowing, the original checkout day is lost from the open Loan, and any lateness is settled as if the copy had really come back. Nothing stops a member from keeping a book this way while other members are waiting for it, or while they owe the library money.
+
+## Solution
+
+A member can **renew** a Loan on a book. Renewing gives the same Loan a new due date: a full loan period counted from the day they renew, worked out exactly like a checkout that day. The Loan keeps its id, its copy, its member and its original borrowing day, and it records how many times it has been renewed.
+
+A Renewal is refused when:
+
+- the Loan is already returned;
+- the copy is a DVD (DVDs are never renewed);
+- the member owes anything at all;
+- the Loan has already been renewed twice;
+- it is more than five days before the due date;
+- someone is waiting for the Title (a Waiting Hold).
+
+An overdue Loan can still be renewed. Renewing it first posts the Fine for the Late days built up so far, as if the copy had come back that day (ADR-0001). Late days then count against the new due date.
+
+Vocabulary follows `GLOSSARY.md`, which already defines **Renewal** in its Loans section. ADR-0001 is not changed by this feature; it already planned for it.
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a member, I want to renew a book I have on loan, so that I can keep reading it without bringing it back to the desk.
+2. As a front-desk staff member, I want a renewal to keep the same Loan, so that the member's loan history shows one Loan per borrowing.
+3. As a front-desk staff member, I want a renewed Loan to keep its original borrowing day, so that the history shows when the member really took the copy out.
+4. As a front-desk staff member, I want the copy to stay on loan through a renewal, so that its status never flickers to the shelf and back.
+5. As a member, I want a renewal to give me a full loan period from the day I renew, so that I get the same time as if I had borrowed the book that day.
+6. As a member, I want a renewed due date that would fall on a Sunday to move to the Monday, so that I'm never due on a day the library is closed.
+7. As a front-desk staff member, I want to see how many times a Loan has been renewed, so that I can tell a member how many renewals they have left.
+8. As a developer calling the service, I want a newly checked-out Loan to show zero renewals, so that the count always starts from the checkout.
+9. As a library manager, I want a Loan to be renewable at most twice, so that one member can't keep a book indefinitely.
+10. As a member who has renewed twice, I want a third renewal to be refused with a clear reason, so that I know I have to bring the book back.
+11. As a library manager, I want DVDs never to be renewable, so that our short-loan stock keeps moving.
+12. As a member asking to renew a DVD, I want to be told that DVDs can't be renewed, even if I also owe money, so that I'm not asked to pay only to be refused again.
+13. As a library manager, I want a renewal to be refused while anyone has a Waiting Hold on the Title, so that members who are queueing get the next copy.
+14. As a member whose Title has only Ready Holds, I want my renewal to go through, so that people who already have their copy on the Hold shelf don't stop me.
+15. As a library manager, I want only members who owe nothing to be able to renew, so that anything owed is paid before a member gets more time.
+16. As a member who owes 0.01, I want to be told I'm Blocked from renewing, with what I owe and the threshold, so that I know exactly what to pay.
+17. As a member who has just paid off their Balance, I want to be able to renew straight away, so that paying unblocks me at once.
+18. As a library manager, I want the same renewal rules for children as for adults, so that there is one rule for renewals.
+19. As a library manager, I want a renewal to be allowed only from five days before the due date, so that members can't renew on the day they borrow and double their loan period.
+20. As a member asking to renew too early, I want a clear refusal, so that I know to come back nearer the due date.
+21. As a member who has renewed once, I want the five-day window to count from my new due date, so that the rule is the same for every renewal.
+22. As a member whose book is overdue, I want to be able to renew it, so that I can keep it legitimately instead of keeping it late.
+23. As a library manager, I want renewing an overdue Loan to post the Fine for the Late days so far, so that lateness never disappears when a due date moves.
+24. As a member who renews an overdue book, I want later Late days to count only from my new due date, so that I'm never fined twice for the same day.
+25. As a library manager, I want the replacement-cost cap to apply to all the Fines on one Loan together, including a Fine posted at renewal, so that a renewed Loan never costs more than the book.
+26. As a member who owes nothing and renews an overdue book, I want the renewal to go through even though it posts a Fine, so that the rule is about what I owe when I ask.
+27. As a library manager, I want a member who has just been fined at renewal to be unable to renew again until they pay, so that the 0.00 rule holds for the next renewal.
+28. As a child member renewing an overdue book, I want no Fine to be posted, so that children are never fined, as everywhere else.
+29. As a member at my loan limit, I want to still be able to renew, so that renewing something I already have isn't treated as borrowing more.
+30. As a front-desk staff member, I want a refused renewal to change nothing, so that a failed request never moves a due date, the count or the Balance.
+31. As a front-desk staff member, I want renewing a returned Loan to be refused as a renewal, so that I'm not told the copy isn't on loan when it may have gone out again to someone else.
+32. As a developer calling the service, I want an unknown loan id to raise `LoanNotFound`, so that error handling is the same as for every other loan lookup.
+33. As a developer calling the service, I want refusals to come in a fixed order, so that the reason a member hears is predictable.
+34. As a front-desk staff member, I want a renewed overdue Loan to drop off the overdue list, so that the overdue report only shows copies that are actually late.
+35. As a front-desk staff member, I want the overdue report to use the renewed due date, so that its days overdue are counted from the date the member was actually given.
+36. As a library manager, I want the renewal limit, the five-day window, the money threshold and the renewable materials kept with the other circulation numbers, so that changing one is a one-line policy change.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+**Interface contract.** Other code will call exactly this:
+
+- `LendingService.renew(loan_id: str) -> Loan`: renews the open Loan in place, with the same `loan_id`, and returns that Loan.
+- `Loan` gains a field `renewals: int`, default `0`: how many times the Loan has been renewed. A new checkout gives `0`. It is the only new field on `Loan`; there is no log of past due dates or renewal days.
+- A new error `RenewalRefused(LendingError)` in the errors module for renewals that can't be granted. It carries the `loan_id` and a short, readable `reason`, and its message includes both, in the style of `HoldNotAllowed`.
+- Existing errors are reused where they fit: `LoanNotFound` for an unknown loan id, and `AccountBlocked` for money owed.
+- `checkout`, `return_copy` and every other existing operation keep their signatures and behaviour.
+
+**Policy**, kept with the other circulation numbers:
+
+- Renewal threshold: 0.01, the same for every member category, next to the borrowing and hold thresholds. Because the Balance is always whole cents, this means "owes anything at all".
+- At most 2 renewals per Loan, the same for every member category.
+- Renewal window: 5 calendar days. A Loan can be renewed on or after its current due date minus 5 days. Closed days are not skipped.
+- Only books are renewable. DVDs never are.
+- The new due date uses the existing due-date rule: the material's loan period from the renewal day, moved to the next open day if it lands on a closed day.
+
+**Renewing.** Checks run in this order. The first one that fails decides the outcome:
+
+1. Loan lookup: `LoanNotFound(loan_id)`.
+2. The Loan is already returned: `RenewalRefused`.
+3. The Loan's Title is not a renewable material (a DVD): `RenewalRefused`.
+4. The renewal block: `AccountBlocked(member_id, balance, 0.01)` when the member's Balance is 0.01 or more. This is checked before any Fine for this renewal is posted.
+5. The Loan has already been renewed twice: `RenewalRefused`.
+6. Today is earlier than the current due date minus 5 days: `RenewalRefused`.
+7. The Title has a Waiting Hold: `RenewalRefused`. Ready Holds don't count.
+
+The DVD check comes before the money check because it is permanent. The limit check comes before the too-early check, so a Loan that has used both renewals says so instead of naming a date.
+
+When every check passes, in this order:
+
+1. The Fine for the Late days up to and including today is posted, dated today and linked to the Loan. The existing fine logic does this, including the cap on the Loan's Fines together and the no-fines rule for children. On or before the due date there are no Late days and nothing is written.
+2. The due date becomes the due date of a checkout of this material today.
+3. `renewals` goes up by one.
+
+The Loan keeps its `loan_id`, `copy_id`, member and `borrowed_on`, and stays open. The copy stays `ON_LOAN`. The loan limit and the borrowing threshold are not checked. Nothing about Holds changes.
+
+A refused renewal changes nothing: no Fine is posted, and the due date, `renewals` and the copy all stay as they were.
+
+**Worked examples** (must hold). The book is borrowed on Monday 2 March 2026 and due Monday 23 March:
+
+- Renewed Thursday 19 March: due Thursday 9 April, renewals 1, no Fine.
+- Renewed Wednesday 18 March: allowed, due Wednesday 8 April.
+- Asked on Tuesday 17 March: `RenewalRefused` (too early).
+- Renewed on Sunday 22 March: 21 days later is Sunday 12 April, so it is due Monday 13 April.
+- After the 19 March renewal (due 9 April), asking on Friday 3 April is too early. Asking on Saturday 4 April is allowed, giving due Saturday 25 April and renewals 2. Any third request is `RenewalRefused` for the limit.
+- An overdue renewal for an adult who owes 0.00, on Saturday 28 March: the Late days are 24–28 March (5 days), so a Fine of 1.25 is posted, dated 28 March. The renewal is granted, due Saturday 18 April. If the book then comes back on Tuesday 21 April, the Late days are 20 and 21 April (19 April is a Sunday), so a second Fine of 0.50 is posted. The Balance is 1.75 if nothing was paid.
+
+**Unchanged:**
+
+- ADR-0001, Fines on return, the Balance, Payments, Manual charges.
+- Checkout, loan periods, loan limits and the borrowing and hold thresholds.
+- Everything about Holds.
+- `loan_history`, `open_loans` and `overdue_loans`, which read the Loan as it now stands. A renewed Loan appears once, with its new due date and count.
+- The overdue report, which keeps counting calendar days since the Loan's current due date.
+
+**Repository:** no change. `renew` reads the Loan by id and changes it in place, as `return_copy` does. The service remains the only thing that changes state.
+
+**Docs:**
+
+- The README's Concepts section gains a short Renewal entry, consistent with `GLOSSARY.md`.
+- The README's Loan entry mentions the renewals count.
+- The README's Blocked entry mentions the 0.01 renewal threshold.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam: the public `LendingService` interface**, built from the shared `service` and `clock` fixtures: an `InMemoryRepository` and a `FixedClock` starting Monday 2 March 2026, with book `978-1` (copies `B1` and `B2`), DVD `dvd-1` (copy `D1`), adult `ann` and child `kid`. The shared fixtures stay unchanged. Tests that need more members or Titles register them themselves. No new seams.
+- **A good test** drives the service the way the desk would: `checkout`, move the clock, `renew`, and where needed `return_copy`, `place_hold`, `add_charge` and `pay`. It asserts only on what the service returns or raises:
+  - the Loan `renew` returns;
+  - `get_loan`, `loan_history`, `open_loans` and `overdue_loans`;
+  - `get_copy(...).status` and `balance()`;
+  - the errors.
+
+  Tests don't call policy functions or reach into the repository, so the limit, the window, the threshold and the date arithmetic are all checked through `renew`.
+- **Prior art:**
+  - the loan tests, for due dates, Sundays and `clock.set(...)` / `clock.advance(...)` with explicit dates;
+  - the fines tests, for Late days that leave out Sundays and for the replacement-cost cap;
+  - the blocked-member tests, for asserting on `AccountBlocked`'s member, Balance and threshold;
+  - the holds tests, for setting up Waiting and Ready Holds.
+- **Behaviours to cover:**
+  - A renewal:
+    - returns the same Loan (same `loan_id`, `copy_id`, member and `borrowed_on`), still open, with renewals 1 and the new due date;
+    - is what `get_loan` then shows;
+    - leaves `loan_history` with one Loan and the copy `ON_LOAN`.
+  - A new checkout has renewals 0.
+  - Every worked example above, including the Sunday case and the second renewal's window.
+  - The window edges: refused 6 days before the due date, allowed 5 days before, on the due date, and when overdue.
+  - The limit: a third renewal is `RenewalRefused`, and the Loan is unchanged.
+  - DVDs: `RenewalRefused` on any day, including the due date, with a Balance of 0.00.
+  - Money:
+    - `AccountBlocked` carrying the member, Balance and 0.01 when the Balance is 0.01, and when it is above that;
+    - allowed after paying back to 0.00;
+    - the same for a child.
+  - Holds:
+    - `RenewalRefused` while the Title has a Waiting Hold;
+    - allowed when the Title's only Holds are Ready.
+  - A returned Loan gives `RenewalRefused` (not `NoOpenLoan`), even after its copy has gone out again on a new Loan.
+  - An unknown id gives `LoanNotFound`.
+  - Overdue renewals:
+    - the Fine is posted at renewal and counts Late days only, with Sundays left out;
+    - a later late return fines only the Late days after the new due date;
+    - the cap holds across both Fines, using a Title with a small replacement cost, after which no further Fine is posted;
+    - a child renewing an overdue book is not fined;
+    - the renewed Loan leaves `overdue_loans`.
+  - A member who owed 0.00, renewed an overdue Loan and was fined is then `AccountBlocked` on their next renewal.
+  - A member at their loan limit can renew.
+  - Check order, where the error types differ:
+    - a returned Loan of a member who owes money gives `RenewalRefused`;
+    - a DVD Loan of a member who owes money gives `RenewalRefused`;
+    - a member who owes money gives `AccountBlocked` even when the Loan is also at its limit, too early, or has a Waiting Hold on its Title.
+  - Check order between `RenewalRefused` reasons (limit before too-early): the `reason` names the limit.
+  - A refused renewal changes nothing: the due date, the count and the Balance stay the same, including for an overdue Loan refused because of a Waiting Hold.
+  - The existing tests pass unchanged.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Renewing DVDs, or any per-material renewal limits other than "books only".
+- Different renewal limits, windows or thresholds per member category.
+- Checking who asks for the renewal. `renew` takes only a loan id; staff renew on the member's behalf.
+- A log of each renewal (past due dates, renewal days). The count is the only record.
+- Renewal fees, automatic renewals, and telling members a renewal is possible or has happened (notifications).
+- Recalling a renewed copy when someone places a Hold after the renewal. The Hold only stops the next renewal.
+- Blocking renewals because the member is holding other overdue copies, as opposed to because of their Balance.
+- Changing ADR-0001, how Fines are posted on return, or the overdue report.
+- Persistence other than the in-memory repository.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- **The Balance is checked before the renewal's Fine is posted.** The rule is a Balance of 0.00 "when they ask". Otherwise an overdue adult Loan could never be renewed. This settles the open question in the holds spec. Confirmed with the user.
+- **Money refusals reuse `AccountBlocked`** with a 0.01 renewal threshold, as Holds reuse it at 5.00. This settles the holds spec's other open question. Confirmed with the user.
+- **ADR-0001 is fulfilled, not changed.** Its "renewals of overdue Loans (planned)" consequence is exactly this feature, and the fine logic already caps the total of a Loan's Fines with more than one Fine in mind.
+- Because a renewal is allowed at most 5 days before the due date, and the loan period for books is 21 days, a renewal always moves the due date at least 16 days later. No special case is needed for a renewal that wouldn't move it.
+- `renew` doesn't check whether the library is open that day, just as `checkout` doesn't. A Sunday renewal (in tests) simply gets its due date moved to Monday.
+- All dates come from the injected Clock. Package code never calls `date.today()`.
