@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Fines are posted when lateness ends, not while it builds up
+
+A Fine goes on a member's Account at the moment a late Loan is settled, when the copy comes back. Until then the Balance doesn't include it. So a member can keep a DVD six weeks overdue, owe 0.00 and still borrow, and that is deliberate. The other option was a Balance that includes Fines still building up on open Loans. We rejected it so that a Balance only ever moves when something is recorded: a Charge or a Payment. That keeps `balance()` the same number for every caller on a given day, and it leaves the borrowing block (and the later, lower hold block) depending on money actually owed, not on an estimate. Blocking members who are holding overdue copies is a separate rule to add if we want it. Don't fold it into the Balance.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Lateness must never disappear. Any operation that moves a Loan's due date has to post the Fine built up so far, counted as if the copy had been returned that day. That means renewals of overdue Loans (planned) must do this. After that, Late days count against the new due date. One Loan can therefore carry more than one Fine.
+- The replacement-cost cap applies to the total of a Loan's Fines, not to each Fine. A later Fine is reduced by whatever earlier Fines on the same Loan already charged. Once the cap is reached, further Late days cost nothing and no Fine is recorded.
+- The overdue report keeps counting calendar days since the due date. A Fine counts Late days, which leave out days the library is closed. The two numbers differ on purpose.
